          <h2>Lesson 1: An Introduction to Biological Rhythms in Animals</h2>

          <p class="section-intro">
            Most likely something woke you up this morning. Maybe an alarm, maybe the light, maybe
            nothing at all &mdash; you just woke up. That last option is the interesting one. Your
            body had an opinion about what time it was <em>before</em> you did. That opinion was
            generated by an internal clock that has been ticking inside you since before you were
            born. Almost every animal has one. This week we look at how those clocks work, how we
            measure them, and why having one matters for survival.
          </p>

          <div class="key-concept">
            <h4>The Big Picture</h4>
            <p>
              The world cycles predictably. Day follows night every 24 hours. The tide rises and
              falls every 12.4 hours. The moon waxes and wanes every 29.5 days. The seasons turn
              every 365.25 days. An animal that can <em>anticipate</em> these cycles is better
              prepared than an animal that simply reacts to them. Evolution has built internal
              biological clocks &mdash; called <strong>endogenous rhythms</strong> &mdash; that
              let animals do exactly that.
            </p>
          </div>


          <!-- ── Cycles in the environment ── -->
          <h3 id="cycles">The World Has Predictable Cycles</h3>

          <p>
            All biological rhythms are responses to cycles that exist outside the organism. Three
            astronomical relationships generate the cycles that matter to almost every animal on
            Earth:
          </p>

          <div class="pathway-diagram fade-in-up">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 800 360" class="pathway-svg" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of the three predictable astronomical cycles: Earth rotation gives the 24-hour day, Moon orbit gives the 12.4-hour tide and 29.5-day lunar phase, Earth orbit gives the 365-day year">
