          </div>


          <!-- ── Four rhythm classes ── -->
          <h3 id="four-classes">The Four Rhythm Classes</h3>

          <p>
            Biologists name the four big rhythm classes after the cycle they track. The Latin word
            <em>circa</em> means &ldquo;about&rdquo;, so a <strong>circa</strong>dian rhythm is one
            with a period of <em>about</em> one day. The deliberate vagueness matters: internal
            clocks rarely tick at <em>exactly</em> the same period as the environmental cycle. They
            sit close, and the environment fine-tunes them.
          </p>

          <div class="rhythm-table-wrap">
            <table class="rhythm-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Rhythm</th><th>Period</th><th>Tracks</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Circadian</strong></td>
                  <td>~24 hours</td>
                  <td>Day&ndash;night cycle</td>
                  <td>Sleep&ndash;wake cycle, kiwi emerging at dusk, body temperature</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Circatidal</strong></td>
                  <td>~12.4 hours</td>
                  <td>Tidal cycle</td>
                  <td>Bivalves opening their shells, fiddler crab activity</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Circalunar</strong></td>
                  <td>~29.5 days</td>
                  <td>Moon phase</td>
                  <td>Coral mass spawning, palolo worm swarming</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><strong>Circannual</strong></td>
                  <td>~365 days</td>
                  <td>Seasonal photoperiod</td>
                  <td>Bird migration, tuatara hibernation, deer antler cycle</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="exam-tip">
            <h4>Exam Tip</h4>
            <p>
              When you name a rhythm in an answer, name the <strong>class</strong>
              (circadian / circatidal / circalunar / circannual) <em>and</em> the
              <strong>period</strong> (~24 h / ~12.4 h / ~29.5 d / ~365 d). Markers want both.
            </p>
          </div>


          <!-- ── The four words ── -->
          <h3 id="vocab">The Four Words That Examiners Want You To Use</h3>

          <p>
            Four operational terms come up in nearly every AS 91603 question about biological
            rhythms. Mixing them up is the most common way students lose marks here. Lock them
            down now.
          </p>

          <div class="vocab-grid">
            <div class="vocab-card">
              <h4>Endogenous rhythm</h4>
              <p>
                A rhythm that originates <em>inside</em> the organism. The internal clock keeps
                ticking even if all external cues are removed. We test this by putting an animal
                in constant darkness and constant temperature: if the rhythm persists, it&rsquo;s
                endogenous.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vocab-card">
              <h4>Exogenous rhythm</h4>
              <p>
                A rhythm driven <em>only</em> by an external cycle. Remove the external cue and
                the rhythm stops. Pure exogenous rhythms are rare in animals &mdash; most apparent
                rhythms turn out to have an endogenous component when properly tested.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vocab-card">
              <h4>Zeitgeber</h4>
              <p>
                German for &ldquo;time giver&rdquo;. An external cue that an organism uses to
                <em>reset</em> its internal clock. For most animals the dominant zeitgeber is the
                light&ndash;dark cycle. Temperature, food availability, and tidal wave-action can
                also act as zeitgebers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="vocab-card">
              <h4>Entrainment</h4>
              <p>
                The process by which a zeitgeber drags an endogenous rhythm into step with the
                environmental cycle. Without entrainment, the clock would drift away from local
                time over weeks or months.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="key-concept key-concept--quote">
            <h4>The Sentence You Need To Be Able To Write</h4>
            <p class="exam-sentence">
              &ldquo;Circadian rhythms are <strong>endogenous</strong> in origin and
              <strong>entrained</strong> to a 24-hour period by <strong>exogenous zeitgebers</strong>,
              principally the light&ndash;dark cycle.&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p>
              Memorise that line. Variations of it work for circatidal, circalunar and circannual
              rhythms too &mdash; just swap the period and the zeitgeber.
            </p>
          </div>


          <!-- ── Mammalian clock ── -->
          <h3 id="mammal-clock">The Mammalian Clock: Eye → SCN → Pineal → Melatonin</h3>

          <p>
            In mammals (including you), the master circadian clock sits in a tiny paired structure
            called the <strong>suprachiasmatic nucleus</strong>, or <strong>SCN</strong>. It is
            located in the hypothalamus, directly above the optic chiasm where the two optic
            nerves cross. Light information reaches the SCN through specialised light-sensitive
            ganglion cells in the retina. The SCN then drives the <strong>pineal gland</strong>,
            which secretes the hormone <strong>melatonin</strong>.
          </p>

          <div class="pathway-diagram fade-in-up">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 800 380" class="pathway-svg" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of the mammalian circadian pathway: light enters the eye, signals travel via the retinohypothalamic tract to the SCN in the hypothalamus, the SCN signals the pineal gland which releases melatonin at night">

              <!-- Title -->
              <text x="400" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-primary)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">The Mammalian Circadian Pathway</text>

              <!-- Stage 1: Eye -->
              <rect x="30" y="60" width="160" height="80" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
              <text x="110" y="86" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">1. EYE (retina)</text>
              <text x="110" y="106" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">Light-sensitive</text>
              <text x="110" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">ganglion cells detect</text>
              <text x="110" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">background light levels</text>

              <!-- Arrow 1 -->
              <line x1="190" y1="100" x2="240" y2="100" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" opacity="0.8"/>
              <polygon points="235,93 248,100 235,107" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.8"/>

              <!-- Stage 2: SCN -->
              <rect x="250" y="60" width="200" height="80" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
              <text x="350" y="86" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">2. SCN</text>
              <text x="350" y="106" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">Suprachiasmatic nucleus</text>
              <text x="350" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">of the hypothalamus</text>
              <text x="350" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">The master clock</text>

              <!-- Arrow 2 -->
              <line x1="450" y1="100" x2="500" y2="100" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.6" opacity="0.8"/>
              <polygon points="495,93 508,100 495,107" fill="var(--accent)" opacity="0.8"/>

              <!-- Stage 3: Pineal -->
              <rect x="510" y="60" width="170" height="80" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="var(--standard-3-colour, #ff6ec7)" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.7"/>
              <text x="595" y="86" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--standard-3-colour, #ff6ec7)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">3. PINEAL GLAND</text>
              <text x="595" y="106" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">Receives SCN signal</text>
              <text x="595" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">Releases melatonin</text>
              <text x="595" y="136" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="11" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">into the bloodstream</text>

              <!-- Down arrow from pineal -->
              <line x1="595" y1="140" x2="595" y2="180" stroke="var(--standard-3-colour, #ff6ec7)" stroke-width="1.6" opacity="0.8"/>
              <polygon points="588,175 595,188 602,175" fill="var(--standard-3-colour, #ff6ec7)" opacity="0.8"/>

              <!-- Stage 4: Melatonin curve label -->
              <text x="595" y="208" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-primary)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">Melatonin over 24 h</text>

              <!-- Melatonin curve axes -->
              <line x1="440" y1="340" x2="760" y2="340" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.6"/>
              <line x1="450" y1="230" x2="450" y2="345" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.6"/>

              <!-- Day/Night shading -->
              <rect x="450" y="230" width="80" height="110" fill="var(--text-muted)" opacity="0.18"/>
              <rect x="680" y="230" width="80" height="110" fill="var(--text-muted)" opacity="0.18"/>

              <!-- Melatonin curve -->
              <path d="M 450 250 Q 490 248 530 270 Q 570 330 605 332 Q 640 330 680 270 Q 720 248 760 250"
                    fill="none" stroke="var(--standard-3-colour, #ff6ec7)" stroke-width="2.2" opacity="0.95"/>

              <!-- X axis labels -->
              <text x="450" y="358" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">night</text>
              <text x="605" y="358" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">noon</text>
              <text x="760" y="358" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">night</text>
              <text x="445" y="240" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif" text-anchor="end">high</text>
              <text x="445" y="338" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif" text-anchor="end">low</text>

              <!-- Side note -->
              <rect x="30" y="200" width="380" height="140" rx="10" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="var(--border-subtle)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.9"/>
              <text x="50" y="225" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">Reading the diagram</text>
              <text x="50" y="248" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="12" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">• Melatonin is HIGH at night and LOW in daylight.</text>
              <text x="50" y="270" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="12" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">• The pattern is not a direct response to light &mdash;</text>
              <text x="50" y="286" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="12" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">  the SCN drives it. The pattern would continue</text>
              <text x="50" y="302" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="12" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">  for days in constant darkness.</text>
              <text x="50" y="324" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="12" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">• Light <tspan font-weight="700">resets</tspan> the SCN. That is entrainment.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>

          <div class="key-concept">
            <h4>Why melatonin matters</h4>
            <p>
              Melatonin is sometimes called &ldquo;the hormone of darkness&rdquo;. Its presence in
              the bloodstream tells every cell in the body that it is nighttime. That signal then
              drives downstream rhythms: body temperature, hormone secretion, alertness, and
              hunger. If you have ever taken melatonin tablets for jet lag, you were using it as a
              <em>chronobiotic</em> &mdash; a substance that shifts the timing of the internal
              clock &mdash; not as a sleeping pill.
            </p>
          </div>


          <!-- ── Activity patterns ── -->
          <h3 id="patterns">Diurnal, Nocturnal, Crepuscular</h3>

          <p>
            All circadian animals fit one of three behavioural categories, based on <em>when</em>
            in the 24-hour cycle they are most active. Each pattern has its own adaptive logic.
          </p>

          <div class="pathway-diagram fade-in-up">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 800 280" class="pathway-svg" role="img" aria-label="Three activity-pattern graphs showing diurnal, nocturnal and crepuscular activity over a 24-hour cycle">

              <!-- ===== Diurnal ===== -->
              <g transform="translate(20,30)">
                <text x="120" y="0" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="13" font-weight="700" font-family="'Space Grotesk', sans-serif">Diurnal</text>
                <text x="120" y="18" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-secondary)" font-size="10" font-family="'Inter', sans-serif">active by day &mdash; e.g. kererū</text>
                <!-- Night shading (left + right) -->
                <rect x="20" y="40" width="40" height="120" fill="var(--text-muted)" opacity="0.18"/>
                <rect x="180" y="40" width="40" height="120" fill="var(--text-muted)" opacity="0.18"/>
                <!-- Axes -->
                <line x1="20" y1="160" x2="220" y2="160" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.6"/>
                <line x1="20" y1="40" x2="20" y2="160" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.6"/>
                <!-- Curve: low, rise, plateau, fall, low -->
                <path d="M 20 155 L 55 152 Q 80 80 120 60 Q 160 80 185 152 L 220 155"
                      fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
                <!-- Labels -->
