          <!-- ── Why have a clock at all ── -->
          <h3 id="why-clock">Why Have a Clock at All?</h3>

          <p>
            A purely reactive animal could, in principle, simply wait for the sun to set before
            starting its night activity. Why bother carrying an internal clock around? Three
            adaptive benefits explain it &mdash; and these are exactly the points NZQA wants you
            to write about when an Excellence question asks for the &ldquo;adaptive advantage&rdquo;.
          </p>

          <div class="info-cards-row">
            <div class="info-card info-card--anticipation">
              <h4>1. Anticipation</h4>
              <p>
                A clock lets the animal <em>prepare</em> for the next phase of its day before it
                arrives. A nocturnal forager wakes and physiologically warms up <em>before</em>
                sunset, so it&rsquo;s ready to feed the moment prey emerges. A reactive animal
                would still be warming up while competitors are already eating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card info-card--coordination">
              <h4>2. Coordination</h4>
              <p>
                Many behaviours only work if every member of the population does them at the same
                time: mass spawning, courtship displays, swarming flights. A shared zeitgeber
                entraining everyone&rsquo;s internal clock keeps the population synchronised
                automatically.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card info-card--robustness">
              <h4>3. Robustness</h4>
              <p>
                A clock keeps ticking through a cloudy day, a brief disturbance, or a temporary
                lab blackout. An animal that depended purely on the sunset cue would be thrown off
                by anything that obscured the cue. The internal clock provides resilience.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>


