          <!-- ── Check yourself ── -->
          <h3 id="check">Check Yourself</h3>

          <p>
            Five quick questions. Try them before you check the answers. If you can&rsquo;t do
            them, scroll back up. If you can do them, you&rsquo;re ready for Lesson 2.
          </p>

          <div class="check-grid">
            <details class="check-card">
              <summary>1. Define &ldquo;zeitgeber&rdquo; in one sentence.</summary>
              <p>An external environmental cue that entrains an organism&rsquo;s endogenous biological rhythm to match the local environmental cycle.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="check-card">
              <summary>2. Classify each rhythm: (a) coral spawning at full moon, (b) bivalves opening their shells as the tide returns, (c) kiwi emerging at dusk, (d) tuatara hibernating in winter.</summary>
              <p>(a) circalunar (~29.5 days). (b) circatidal (~12.4 hours). (c) circadian (~24 hours, crepuscular activity pattern). (d) circannual (~365 days).</p>
            </details>
            <details class="check-card">
              <summary>3. What is the difference between an endogenous and an exogenous rhythm? How would you tell them apart experimentally?</summary>
              <p>An endogenous rhythm originates inside the organism and continues even when external cues are removed. An exogenous rhythm is driven only by the external cue and stops if the cue is removed. The test is to put the organism in constant conditions (constant darkness, constant temperature, no time-of-day cues) and see if the rhythm persists. If it does, it&rsquo;s endogenous.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="check-card">
              <summary>4. Trace the mammalian circadian pathway from light reaching the eye to melatonin in the blood. Use four named structures.</summary>
              <p>Light is detected by light-sensitive ganglion cells in the <strong>retina</strong>. Signals travel along the retinohypothalamic tract to the <strong>SCN</strong> in the hypothalamus. The SCN signals the <strong>pineal gland</strong>, which releases <strong>melatonin</strong> into the bloodstream &mdash; high at night, low during the day.</p>
            </details>
            <details class="check-card">
              <summary>5. Give the Excellence-grade reason an animal benefits from having an internal clock rather than just reacting to the sunset.</summary>
              <p>Anticipation: the animal can prepare physiologically for the next environmental phase before it arrives, so it&rsquo;s ready to forage / breed / shelter at the optimal moment. A purely reactive animal would always be one step behind, allowing competitors with clocks to out-compete it. (Coordination and robustness are also valid second points.)</p>
            </details>
          </div>


