          <h2>Lesson 2: Reading Actograms and Free-Running Periods</h2>

          <p class="section-intro">
            Once you know an animal has a clock, the next question is: <em>how do you measure
            it?</em> Biologists use a chart called an <strong>actogram</strong>. Each row plots one
            24-hour day of activity. Once you can read one, you can extract the rhythm&rsquo;s
            period, watch it drift in constant conditions, and calculate a free-running period to
            the nearest minute.
          </p>

          <div class="preview-list">
            <p><strong>What we&rsquo;ll cover:</strong></p>
            <ul class="feature-list">
              <li>How to read a single- and double-plotted actogram</li>
              <li>Identifying the moment a zeitgeber is removed</li>
              <li>Calculating phase shift per day and free-running period</li>
              <li>Worked example using Scipad pp.35&ndash;37</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>


