          <h2>Lesson 3: Three Case Studies &mdash; NZ Tree Weta, Fiddler Crab, Tuatara</h2>

          <p class="section-intro">
            One animal per rhythm class, each chosen to make the vocabulary come alive. The
            <strong>NZ tree weta</strong> shows a textbook nocturnal circadian rhythm. The
            <strong>fiddler crab</strong> gives us the cleanest circatidal actogram in biology.
            The <strong>tuatara</strong> demonstrates a NZ-endemic circannual rhythm, with the
            bonus weirdness of its &ldquo;third eye&rdquo; on the top of its head.
          </p>

          <div class="preview-list">
            <p><strong>What we&rsquo;ll cover:</strong></p>
            <ul class="feature-list">
              <li>NZ tree weta &mdash; circadian, nocturnal, light-dark zeitgeber</li>
              <li>Fiddler crab &mdash; circatidal, dual-peak actogram, mechanical-cue zeitgeber</li>
              <li>Tuatara &mdash; circannual, hibernation, parietal eye and the pineal gland</li>
              <li>The Ralph 1990 hamster SCN-transplant experiment &mdash; how we know the clock is in the SCN</li>
            </ul>
